Itk in bext
===========

Tcl 8.6.18 already bundles Itcl 4.3.7, so bext deliberately has no separate
Itcl project.  Itk is not part of either source distribution, so the bundled
stack builds Itk from its own upstream repository after Tcl and Tk.  A system
Tcl/Tk stack must already provide Itcl and Itk; bext never builds Itk against
system Tcl/Tk.

Itk cannot be built from installed public headers alone.  Its implementation
includes Tcl's private tclInt.h and Itcl's private itclInt.h and directly uses
the corresponding internal structures and entry points.  The Itk build must
therefore use the private headers and stubs from the exact Tcl/Itcl sources it
accompanies.  Bext supplies the standard install-sh helper omitted from the Itk
repository.  On Windows, explicit Tcl, Tk, and Itcl source directories are
passed to nmake.  On Unix-like platforms, upstream's generated TEA configure
logic gets the corresponding source locations from the bundled configuration.

Upstream's generic Windows TEA install target copies only ``*.tcl`` library
files.  Itk also needs its ``*.itk`` class definitions and ``tclIndex`` at
runtime, so bext extends the nmake install target to copy them and verifies the
complete script set before accepting the install.

Itk itself makes no direct Xlib calls: all of its window-system interaction
goes through Tk stubs.  Upstream's TEA configuration nevertheless adds the
host X library to Itk.  Bext suppresses that unnecessary dependency so Itk
uses the X implementation selected by its matching Tk build and does not mix
two incompatible X ABIs in builds such as the Xmin test configuration.

Itk's own nominal Itcl 4.1 requirement is not sufficient: its upstream suite
has seven failures with Itcl 4.1.0, involving private/protected components and
destroyed-component cleanup, while all 100 tests pass with Itcl 4.1.1.  Bext
therefore retains 4.1.1 as Itk's stub and package-index floor.

BRL-CAD has a stronger system-stack requirement.  With Tcl/Tk 8.6.18, Itcl
4.1.1, 4.1.2, and 4.2.3 fail the Tk-first Iwidgets baseline because they lack
Itcl 4.3.0's workaround for Tcl 8.6.15's `Tcl_NewObjectInstance` behavior.
The complete baseline passes with Itcl 4.3.0 and Itk 4.2.3, so bext requires
Itcl 4.3.0 or newer before accepting a system stack.

System selection is all-or-nothing: Tcl must load Itcl 4.3.0 or newer, and its
package path must advertise Itk 4.2.3 or newer before bext accepts the matching
system Tk.  Itk detection asks Tcl's package handler to index available versions
without executing the selected package's load script, enforcing the floor on
headless builders without trying to create a Tk window.  If either extension is
missing or too old, bext selects the complete bundled stack.

The bundled Itk revision reports version 4.2.7 and includes the upstream August
2024 release plus subsequent Windows, Tcl/Tk 9, warning, and TEA maintenance.
The complete upstream suite and BRL-CAD GUI baseline pass with the bundled
Itcl under Xmin using the Xmin-compatible Tk build.
